\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn a representation by predicting, in embedding space, the representation of a target part of an input from the representation of a context part~\citep{lecun2022path,assran2023ijepa,bardes2024vjepa}.
They use no negative examples and, unlike masked autoencoders~\citep{he2022mae}, reconstruct no pixels; the same objective, however, admits a trivial minimiser: if the encoder maps every input to the same point, every prediction is exact.
More subtly, the representation can collapse \emph{dimensionally}, spanning only a few directions of the embedding space~\citep{jing2022dimensional}, which degrades downstream performance~\citep{garrido2023rankme}.

JEPAs and their non-contrastive relatives~\citep{grill2020byol,chen2021simsiam,baevski2022data2vec,caron2021dino} avoid collapse through design choices: a predictor of a particular depth, width or learning rate; a high masking ratio and a geometry of context and target blocks; a stop-gradient on the target branch with an exponential moving average (EMA) of the online encoder.
Each choice is supported by ablations, and several have partial theoretical explanations~\citep{tian2021understanding,richemond2023edge,halvagal2023implicit,littwin2024jepa}, but there is no single account of \emph{why} these choices work, nor a criterion that predicts \emph{when} a configuration collapses and how much rank it keeps.

We build such an account on one premise: collapse is decided early in training, near the collapsed solution, where a \emph{driving force}, the predictive signal shared between context and target, grows each direction of representation space and a \emph{decay}, from weight decay and from the self-prediction pressure of the loss, shrinks it.
A direction should survive when driving wins, and the rank should count such directions; but with weight decay the collapsed solution is locally stable and the predictor itself decays, so survival is decided by a race between a direction's transient growth and the decay of the predictor, not by the sign of its growth rate at initialisation.

Part of this picture is established: for linear BYOL/SimSiam and for predictive encoders with an optimal predictor, a mode survives weight decay only if its predictive signal exceeds a static threshold~\citep{tian2021understanding,wang2021directset,ponce2025dual,arbel2026peira}, and without the stop-gradient every mode collapses~\citep{tian2021understanding,halvagal2023implicit}.
Those analyses work in a regime where the predictor is as small as the encoder, so the drive is quadratic at the collapsed point.
At a standard initialisation the predictor is of order one while the encoder is small; the drive is then first order, and what decides survival is how far a direction grows before the predictor decays. Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{The race criterion.} Near collapse, each data direction is carried by an amplitude and a predictor gain whose equations are known in substance (\cref{prop:reduction,prop:fixed}). In the unbalanced regime of a standard initialisation we derive when a direction captures the decaying gain before the gain leaves its growth window (\cref{def:race}); the criterion has no fitted constants and locates the separatrix between collapse and survival, which the static threshold does not.
\item \textbf{Predictions.} The criterion gives the rank, a critical weight decay, $\lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx h_k(p_0)/\log(p_0/a_0)$ for $\alpha=1$, far below the static threshold, a rank non-monotone in weight decay and in predictor scale, the need for predictor weight decay whenever the encoder is decayed, and the term through which each heuristic acts (\cref{sec:heuristics}, \cref{tab:heuristics}).
\item \textbf{Tests.} On a development set and on \EoneBRuns{} held-out, pre-registered linear-JEPA runs (\cref{sec:experiments}), the criterion predicts the rank at convergence with error \EoneBAlignedMae{} against \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} for the static threshold, and the collapse boundary in weight decay; we report where it fails (deep predictors, corrected post hoc and re-tested; one EMA sign; AdamW). Under masking it predicts the rank from the mask statistics with error \EtwoCycMae{} in population training (static: \EtwoCycBoundMae{}; SGD: \EtwoSgdMae{}) and fails for a tanh encoder under random masks. In a ViT on CIFAR-10 a step-0 rule orders the configurations of an SGD arm, through its weight-decay sweep, but misplaces the transition, and the predictor, EMA and masking effects do not transfer.
\end{itemize}
